<Base title="Methods — Catalog of Complexity" description="The production pipeline as public-facing credibility: playbook, verifiers, provenance conventions.">
  <h1 class="page-title">Methods: how an entry is made and checked</h1>
  <p class="page-lede">
    The git repo is the database, and the pipeline that fills it is as much a part of the
    catalog's credibility as any individual number. This page describes the playbook, the
    automated verification pipeline, the machine conformance check that gates every build, and
    the validation program run against the finished v0.1 panel — including the parts of that
    program that came back unflattering.
  </p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>How an entry is produced</h2>
    <p>
      Every entry follows the same six-step playbook, proven by hand on the first two pilot
      entries (tropical cyclones, financial markets) before being encoded as an automated
      workflow for the remaining eight:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Scoping note.</strong> Define the system boundary, granularity, and chosen
        exemplar(s) before any research begins — what is inside the system and what is outside
        it. Most catalog quality failures turn out to be scoping failures.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Source sweep.</strong> Build the entry's source list before filling any values:
        the canonical review paper(s), the standard textbook treatment, and the seminal
        complexity-literature papers for that system. Reviews are preferred for values; primary
        papers are preferred for the provenance of famous numbers.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Panel fill.</strong> Column by column against the frozen panel spec. Quantitative
        columns need a published value with unit, method, citation, and confidence. Rubric
        columns are scored against the worked anchors with a justification citing system-specific
        evidence. Anything not found is recorded as <code>unknown</code> with a note — never
        interpolated, never guessed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Adversarial verification.</strong> Four independent agents, each instructed to
        try to refute the draft rather than approve it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Flag triage.</strong> Every flag is routed for review with the verifier's note and
        a proposed resolution; unflagged entries proceed without further human review.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Status bump.</strong> Draft becomes verified once all checks pass or flags are
        resolved, then published at or after public launch.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The four adversarial verifiers</h2>
    <p>
      This is the automated shape encoded in the entry-production workflow: research sweep, then
      draft, then the four verifiers in parallel, then a fixer pass, then — once a whole batch of
      entries exists — a cross-entry audit.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Citation checker.</strong> Assumes every citation is wrong until the source is
        shown to support the claim. A search snippet alone is never a pass; verdicts are pass
        (source read, states the claim), partial (approximately right, with the exact
        discrepancy noted), unverifiable (source unreachable), or fail (source contradicts or
        doesn't contain the claim). Every fail blocks promotion.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Plausibility checker.</strong> Hunts arithmetic errors, unit errors,
        order-of-magnitude contradictions, and internal inconsistencies — re-deriving every
        derivable number, checking timescale nesting, and checking that evidence-status and
        confidence combinations obey the panel spec (a "measured" value needs a source; a
        "derived" value needs a stated method; an unsupported power-law tail must be marked
        measured-untested).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Consistency checker.</strong> The commensurability enforcer: audits every
        rubric or hybrid score against the anchor library, and — where the class is itself a
        worked anchor — requires the score and justification to match the anchor exactly. Also
        audits every filling rule and every level tag, and checks the new entry against the
        already-verified entries for cross-entry coherence.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Coverage checker.</strong> Assumes the entry is incomplete. Checks all 30
        panel-spec columns are present (missing-entirely is a defect; present-but-unknown is
        not), checks every required structural block, and checks the source sweep for
        recovered-but-never-integrated values.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Verifier findings overrule both the draft and the anchors — during the pilot batch, a
      primary read once corrected a worked anchor, not the entry it was checking. Once a whole
      batch of entries lands, a cross-entry commensurability audit reads every entry against the
      anchor scales together, hunts ordering violations (does an entry's rank on a column survive
      a side-by-side reading of its justification against the scale text?), checks that expected
      structural relationships hold, and verifies the relations graph is reciprocal.
    </p>

    <h2>Filling rules</h2>
    <p>These eight rules are binding on both the research agents that draft entries and the verifiers that check them:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>no-guessing</strong> — <code>unknown</code> is always a legal value; interpolation and vibes are not.</li>
      <li><strong>power-law-rigor</strong> — a power-law claim enters the catalog only with CSN-2009-grade statistical support, or is explicitly labeled <code>measured-untested</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>soc-two-claims</strong> — power-law event statistics and self-organized criticality as mechanism are separate claims, recorded separately.</li>
      <li><strong>no-market-chaos</strong> — low-dimensional deterministic chaos in financial markets is debunked; markets get "nonlinear dependence: yes, deterministic chaos: no evidence."</li>
      <li><strong>measurand-required</strong> — signal-relative quantities (entropy rate, temporal fractal dimension, tail exponents) must name their measurand.</li>
      <li><strong>level-required</strong> — every value states whether it attaches at instance or class level.</li>
      <li><strong>atomicity-convention</strong> — "atomic parts" are the lowest-level components at which the system's characteristic dynamics operate (a market's atoms are traders and orders, not neurons), declared per entry and audited for cross-entry consistency.</li>
      <li><strong>interaction-typing</strong> — an interaction type is individuated by mechanism and carrier, not by outcome or sign (buy and sell orders are one type, order flow, not two), and is audited across entries the same way rubric scores are.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The conformance validator</h2>
    <p>
      Every entry must pass <code>analysis/validate.mjs</code>, a machine conformance checker
      that reads the frozen panel spec and confirms structurally that every required column is
      present with the right kind, that rubric scores are in range, that quantitative values
      carry a level and evidence status, that component ontologies and interaction ontologies are
      well-formed, and more — independent of whether any given value is actually correct. This
      validator gates the production build: the Netlify build chain runs it before the analysis
      pipeline and the site build, so a structurally malformed entry cannot ship.
    </p>

    <h2>The v0.2 validation program</h2>
    <p>
      Once the ten-entry pilot panel and its first cross-system analysis existed, a validation
      pass stress-tested the instrument itself, not just individual entries. The results were not
      uniformly flattering, and are reported here as run.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Blind inter-rater reliability study.</strong> Three blind raters (independent Claude
      models at three tiers, with each sampled entry's own worked anchor redacted) rescored five
      entries across all fifteen rubric and hybrid columns, and per-column agreement was computed
      as ordinal Krippendorff's alpha. About a third of the columns — <code>adaptive_capacity</code>,
      <code>chaos_sensitivity</code>, <code>information_processing</code>, and
      <code>self_organization</code> — showed solid to excellent agreement, both with each other
      and with the catalog's own scores. But three anchors came back at or below chance blind
      agreement: <code>criticality</code> (alpha &asymp; &minus;0.27), <code>tipping_transitions</code>
      (alpha &asymp; &minus;0.03), and <code>emergence</code> (alpha = 0.00). In each case the raters
      agreed on the underlying facts but disagreed on how strictly to apply the column's own
      scale boundary — how much informal or single-case evidence is enough to clear a higher
      band. Those anchors have been flagged for tightening rather than treated as
      already-adequate.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Leave-one-out robustness study.</strong> The PCA and correlation pipeline was
      re-run ten times, once with each entry removed, plus perturbation and Monte Carlo checks on
      individual score cells. The strongest correlation in the panel
      (information_processing&harr;adaptive_capacity, r&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.952) held up as robust
      across every leave-one-out run and every perturbation test. But the reported kinship between
      the-internet and earthquake-fault-systems — flagged in the first analysis pass as an
      interesting cross-category pattern — survived only 6 of 10 leave-one-out runs, and has been
      demoted from a finding to an anecdote pending a larger roster.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Three-lens hostile-referee review.</strong> Independent adversarial reviews from a
      statistics/psychometrics lens, a complexity-science lens, and a general hostile-referee
      posture read the frozen instrument, its anchors, and the analysis built on top of it, and
      filed structured findings. The most consequential single result: the reported correlations
      are the largest of roughly 120 pairwise tests run across the score matrix, and at n&nbsp;=&nbsp;10
      only the 0.952 information_processing&harr;adaptive_capacity coupling survives a
      Bonferroni-style correction (critical |r| &asymp; 0.90). The other headline pairs reported in
      the first analysis pass — decentralization&harr;self_organization (0.872),
      information_processing&harr;alphabet_closure (0.868), modularity&harr;self_organization
      (&minus;0.853), and adaptive_capacity&harr;alphabet_closure (0.850) — clear an uncorrected
      significance threshold but fail the corrected one, and are now reported as
      hypothesis-generating rather than statistically established. (The
      adaptive_capacity&harr;alphabet_closure pair is treated somewhat differently because it was
      a pre-registered hypothesis in the frozen panel spec rather than mined after the fact, which
      earns it a partial pass on multiple-comparisons grounds even though it still misses strict
      correction.) The reviews also raised structural questions the catalog has not fully
      resolved — whether four of the dynamics columns (nonlinearity, feedback, chaos_sensitivity,
      criticality) are measuring meaningfully separate things or are highly collinear by
      construction, and whether the frozen panel omits a computational-capability axis outright
      rather than merely deferring it. Both are recorded as open questions rather than settled
      either way.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Prior-art scan.</strong> A dedicated sweep searched for existing attempts to score
      multiple heterogeneous real systems on a common cross-domain complexity instrument. See
      Related work below.
    </p>

    <h2>Related work</h2>
    <p>
      Every individual ingredient in this catalog pre-exists it; the combination is what is new.
      Ladyman, Lambert &amp; Wiesner's feature checklist (<em>What is a complex system?</em>,
      2013, and the 2020 book with Wiesner) is the closest relative by vocabulary — nonlinearity,
      feedback, self-organization, robustness, decentralization, emergence, and hierarchy all
      appear near-verbatim in both instruments, and this catalog's own filling rules cite LLW's
      verdict directly. But LLW built the questionnaire and never administered it: named systems
      appear only as one-off prose illustrations, never as rows scored against the feature list as
      columns. Chaisson's energy-rate-density program (<em>Cosmic Evolution</em>, 2001, and
      subsequent papers) is the closest relative by method discipline applied to non-biological
      systems specifically — a real, citable, cross-system quantitative comparison spanning
      astrophysical through cultural categories — but it is one metric, not a multi-dimensional
      panel; it is one of this catalog's thirty columns rather than a rival instrument. The
      network-science comparative-table genre (Newman's 2003 survey and its descendants) is the
      closest relative by format — systems as rows, quantitative attributes as columns, with
      citations — but it is topology-only, covering a proper subset of one of this catalog's six
      column groups. Seshat, the Global History Databank, is the closest methodological cousin
      for provenance discipline at scale — expert-coded, source-annotated, uncertainty-tagged
      variables across roughly 400 historical polities — though its domain barely overlaps this
      catalog's. No prior attempt at a fixed, frozen, multi-column panel applied uniformly across
      heterogeneous non-biological real systems, with every value's provenance and evidential
      status declared, feeding an actual statistical analysis, turned up in a focused search pass.
      That is a statement about what a bounded search found, not a claim of exhaustive literature
      clearance.
    </p>

    <h2>Provenance conventions</h2>
    <p>
      Every citation carries a read-status annotation — primary-read, snippet-verified, or
      explicitly flagged as unverified model knowledge — so a reader can tell what was actually
      checked from what was recalled. Fix logs and verification records inside each entry are
      append-only: corrections are recorded as new dated entries, not silent overwrites, so the
      evidence trail for every value survives. And <code>unknown</code> is always a legal value.
      Guessing is not. A blank cell with a note is more honest than an interpolated number, and
      the schema is built to make that the easy choice, not the effortful one.
    </p>
  </div>
